title: Comparing numbers
mode: vhdl
chapter: Buses and numbers
check: behaves
---- intro
Processors compare numbers all the time: is the count done, is this bigger than that? In VHDL a comparison is a condition, and `when ... else` turns it into a bit:

```
eq <= '1' when a = b else '0';
```

`=` works on any two buses. To ask which is **bigger**, the bits must be read as numbers, with `unsigned(...)` from `numeric_std`: `unsigned(a) > unsigned(b)`. Without it VHDL does not know whether `"1000"` means 8 or something else.
---- task
`eq` is 1 when `a` and `b` are equal, and `gt` is 1 when `a` is bigger than `b`, both read as numbers from 0 to 15.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity compare4 is
  port(
    a  : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b  : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    eq : out std_logic;
    gt : out std_logic
  );
end compare4;

architecture rtl of compare4 is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;
use ieee.numeric_std.all;

entity compare4 is
  port(
    a  : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    b  : in  std_logic_vector(3 downto 0);
    eq : out std_logic;
    gt : out std_logic
  );
end compare4;

architecture rtl of compare4 is
begin
    eq <= '1' when a = b else '0';
    gt <= '1' when unsigned(a) > unsigned(b) else '0';
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two lines, each `... <= '1' when <condition> else '0';`.
---- hint2
`eq <= '1' when a = b else '0';` and `gt <= '1' when unsigned(a) > unsigned(b) else '0';`
---- solution
```
eq <= '1' when a = b else '0';
gt <= '1' when unsigned(a) > unsigned(b) else '0';
```
For `a = "1001"` (9) and `b = "0110"` (6), `eq` is 0 and `gt` is 1.
---- done
`<`, `<=`, `>=` and `/=` (not equal) work the same way. Next: more tables.
